    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3909A               REVISED MEMO 6/15/2019\n\nSPONSOR: KAMINSKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the executive law, in relation to\npolicies and procedures regarding hate crimes and establishing a hate\ncrimes training program\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to ensure that state and local law enforce-\nment agencies are properly trained in hate crime recognition.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nDirects the municipal police training council in consultation with the\nDivision of Human Rights and the Hate Crimes task force to develop,\nmaintain and disseminate policies and procedures regarding the recogni-\ntion of and response to hate crimes. Additionally, the council shall\nrecommend to the Governor and the legislature rules and regulations with\nrespect to establishing training and retraining of police officers.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nThis version directs the municipal police training council to work with\nDHR and the Hate crimes task force, rather than having DCJS take the\nlead.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThough New York State law provides strong protections from hate crimes,\nanti-Semitism and other crimes based on religion, race, and sexual pref-\nerence are rising at a disconcerting pace. These hate crimes have no\nplace in New York and no community should be subjected to hateful rheto-\nric. More than ever, law enforcement agencies on the front lines of\nresponding to the wave of hate crimes should be supported and engaged in\nefforts to protect all New Yorkers. To ensure protection from these acts\nof violence, officials should be given the proper training on how to\nrecognize and respond to discriminatory practices. Training for all\nstate and local law enforcement agencies would serve to aid our first\nresponders and discourage future hate crimes by creating more account-\nability.  According to the recent Anti-Defamation League (ADL) audit, in\n2017 there have been 1,267 incidents nationwide, 380 in New York alone.\nThis is a 57% rise from 2016 nationally and a 90% increase on the state\nlevel. Passage of this bill would signal that New York is taking appro-\npriate and effective measures to ensure that all New Yorkers, regardless\nof religion, race, or sexual preference, are afforded equal protection\nunder the law.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2018: S8562 (Kaminsky) - Senate Finance\n2018: A10023A (Rozic) - Governmental Operations\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nMinimal\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the 90th day after it shall have become\nlaw.\n",
